---
/**
 * Token Counter tool page.
 *
 * `client:load` rather than `client:visible`: the island *is* the page, and it
 * sits above the fold, so deferring hydration would only add a visible delay.
 */
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import TokenCounter from '../../components/tools/TokenCounter';
---

<ToolLayout slug="token-counter">
  <TokenCounter client:load />

  <Fragment slot="about">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      Two questions come up constantly and neither has a good answer to hand. How big is this
      prompt, really? And what will it cost on the model I am actually going to use, rather than the
      one in the tutorial?
    </p>
    <p>
      The second question is the harder one. Published price lists live on a dozen vendor pages in a
      dozen units: per thousand tokens, per million, per character. The same model also costs
      different amounts through a first-party API, through Bedrock, and through a gateway. Comparing
      them by hand is tedious enough that most people do not, and pick a model on reputation.
    </p>

    <h2>The approach</h2>
    <p>
      The catalogue comes from <a
        href="https://github.com/BerriAI/litellm/blob/main/model_prices_and_context_window.json"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">LiteLLM's price list</a
      >, which covers around 2,400 token-priced models across 80-odd providers and is the most
      complete public source there is. It is trimmed at author time to the fields a cost calculator
      reads, converted to one unit, and committed as a static asset, so the page loads it from this
      origin rather than pulling 1.8 MB from a third party every time someone opens it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Counting is the part that cannot be exact. No production tokenizer ships as a client-side
      library, and the authoritative counters are API endpoints that need a key and a round trip,
      which would mean sending your prompt to someone. So the estimate uses a segment-weighted
      character model instead: text is split into runs of similar character class and each run is
      charged its own characters-per-token rate, which handles the cases a flat <code
        >chars / 4</code
      >
      rule gets badly wrong: code, CJK, long digit strings, runs of whitespace.
    </p>

    <h2>Where it stops being trustworthy</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Expect ±10% against a real tokenizer on prose and ±15% on code. That is fine for budgeting
        and wrong for billing reconciliation.
      </li>
      <li>
        Prices are list prices. Batch discounts, committed-use rates, priority tiers and per-request
        surcharges such as web search are all excluded.
      </li>
      <li>
        The cache slider prices cached input at the model's published cache-read rate. It does not
        model cache <em>writes</em>, which bill above the input rate, so a cold first pass costs
        more than the figure shown.
      </li>
      <li>
        A snapshot goes stale. The date under the table is the date the price list was taken, not
        today.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
